[2] is a(P)/b(P) MONOTONE, with the worst case at P=1?  (the memo flags this as unchecked)
   decreasing over P=1..4000 : True
   rho(1) = 0.491977   rho(10) = 0.349869   rho(4000) = 0.333375   limit 1/3 = 0.333333
   so c_* = sup rho = rho(1) = log(9/7)/log(5/3) = 0.491977

[3] IDENTIFICATION.  With s = 8P (the reading), expand for large P:
   P=   10: a=0.02500130 vs 2/s=0.02500000   b=0.07145896 vs 6/s=0.07500000
   P=  100: a=0.00250000 vs 2/s=0.00250000   b=0.00746272 vs 6/s=0.00750000
   P= 1000: a=0.00025000 vs 2/s=0.00025000   b=0.00074963 vs 6/s=0.00075000
   => a(P) ~ 2/s and b(P) ~ 6/s.  These ARE inequalities (i) and (iii) of LONG_RETURN_CIRCLE_v1.

[4] THE COEFFICIENT.  Each runner's return intervals are DISJOINT and tile the run, so
    sum_i b(P_i^x) <= L_N for each x, and over 7 runners sum_x sum_i b <= 7 L_N -- and that
    is TIGHT when all seven set throughout.  Then S_N <= A + 7*c_* L_N.
    c_* at P=1         c_*=0.4920  ->  7*c_* = 3.4438   FAILS (needs < 1)
    c_* asymptotic     c_*=0.3333  ->  7*c_* = 2.3333   FAILS (needs < 1)
    multiplicity needed for closure at c_*=0.4920: mu < 2.033
